Here's an example of JavaScript code that checks if a prediction contains width and height and removes any predictions that do not have a square shape within a 10% ratio:

```
// Example predictions containing width and height
let predictions = [
  {
    width: 100,
    height: 100,
    ratio: 1
  },
  {
    width: 150,
    height: 120,
    ratio: 1.2
  },
  {
    width: 200,
    height: 150,
    ratio: 1.33
  }
];

// Check if the ratio is within 10%
for (let i = 0; i < predictions.length; i++) {
  let ratio = predictions[i].ratio;
  if (ratio < 0.9 || ratio > 1.1) {
    console.log("Removing prediction " + i + " because ratio is not within 10%");
    predictions.splice(i, 1);
  }
}

// Final predictions array
console.log(predictions);
```

In this example, the code checks the `ratio` property of each prediction and removes any predictions that have a ratio outside of the 10% range (0.9 to 1.1). The final predictions array should only contain predictions that are square-shaped within a 10% ratio.
